  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1243 AM EDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nAs high pressure ridging continues to build into the region the rest \nof this week, expect the lake effect cloud cover seen overhead early \nthis morning to progressively erode away across the area today, with \nmostly sunny skies seen during the daylight hours. While we can \nexpect weak WAA to slowly work its way into the region today, with \nantecedent cooler air remaining overhead, expect the high \ntemperatures today to range from the 40s along the Lake Superior \nshoreline to the 50s pretty much everywhere else. With the building \nridging bringing calm winds to the sfc tonight, expect lows to tank \ninto the 20s in the interior and 30s near the Great Lakes. \n\nWhile a subtle lower atmospheric shortwave moves through the Upper \nMidwest and Upper Great Lakes this weekend, rain chances associated \nwith it aren't that impressive as mid to upper level guidance \nsuggests that some subsidence could be seen in these portions of the \natmosphere. In addition, the forcing associated with the shortwave \nin the lower levels looks to be weak; without much instability to \nassist, there's a 60-70% chance that the area remains dry for this \nweekend, even as cloud skies move overhead. That being said, expect \nthe temperatures to keep warming Saturday and Sunday, with highs \nSaturday getting into the 50s and Sunday into the lower 60s. Expect \nlows Saturday and Sunday nights to be notably warmer too, only \ndropping down into the 40s Saturday night and only into the mid 40s \nSunday night, even in the traditional 'cold spots' in the interior \nareas. \n\nMore dry weather is seen next Monday as sfc ridging builds to around \n1024-1026mb around Upper Michigan. However, better rain chances are \nnow projected to move into the area around the middle of next week, \nstarting as early as late next Monday as a vertically-stacked low \nlifts through the Plains into northern Ontario. Given the model \nconvergence recently on this solution, confidence is increasing on \nthis bringing rainfall to the area for the middle of next week. \nShould the current modeled solutions hold, we could see a wetting \n(quarter inch or greater) rainfall across the area between the \nmiddle and end of next week. In addition, the model convergence is \nalso increasing the likelihood of well above normal temperatures \nmoving overhead for the last half of the forecast period.",
